8353450,2024-02-15,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"ACCOUNT NAME : XXXX XXXX ACCOUNT NUMBER : XXXX It is enshrined in the annals of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, under the category of 15 USC 1681 section 602 ( a ), that consumer reporting agencies have the solemn duty of guarding the fairness, impartiality, and respect for consumer privacy in their line of work. In this scheme of stakeholders, esteemed institutions such as XXXX, XXXX, and Experian operate as consumer reporting agencies and I, the undersigned, fall under the category of the consumer. 

In this carefully orchestrated dance of finance and privacy, my right not to have my-personal information unjustly shared, finds backing in 15 USC 6801 . The clause articulates that every financial institution, ( which, in this case, includes the Furnisher of information to credit agencies ), has an unyielding obligation to maintain the sanctity of their customers ' privacy and to shield their nonpublic personal information. 

Yet again turning to the tenets of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, the section 604 subsection ( a ) .2 asserts the power of consent. It states that a consumer report can only be furnished under certain circumstances, the most crucial among them being the written consent of the consumer. In present circumstances, both the consumer reporting agencies XXXX and Experian, and the furnisher of information to credit agencies, lack any such endorsement from me ; neither in written nor in any other form. Henceforth, any form of consent to the aforementioned parties, be it verbal, non-verbal, written, implied, or otherwise, stands unequivocally revoked. 

The ethos of 15 USC 6802 ( b ) ( c ) stipulate that a financial institution, such as the furnisher of information to credit agencies, can not disclose nonpublic personal information to a nonaffiliated third party without informing the customer of their nondisclosure option. Yet, I was never enlightened of my right to exercise this option. 

Inflicting another breach, they reported an adverse account in violation of 15 USC 1681C ( a ) ( 5 ), which clearly states that reporting agencies can not publish any adverse item of information without specific authorization. This includes items which predate the report by more than seven years. 

The guidelines mentioned under 15 U.S. Code 1681s2 ( A ) ( 1 ) A prohibit the furnishing of any information that is known or believed to be inaccurate. Yet, contravening the law has become a frequent occurrence with these reporting agencies.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,Experian Information Solutions Inc.,TX,76119,,Web,2024-02-15,Closed with explanation,Yes
