8356979,2024-02-16,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"According to Section 602 ( a ) of the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( 15 USC 1681 ), it is imperative to ensure that consumer reporting agencies fulfill their significant duties with fairness, impartiality, and a regard for consumers ' privacy rights. XXXX and XXXX are recognized as consumer reporting agencies, and I, as the consumer, possess the inherent right to safeguard my private information, as supported by Section 6801 of Title 15 of the United States Code, which stipulates the Congress 's policy regarding financial institutions ' obligation to uphold customer privacy and confidentiality.

Under Section 604 ( a ) ( 2 ) of Title 15 USC 1681, a consumer reporting agency may furnish a consumer report solely in accordance with the written instructions of the concerned consumer, thereby requiring explicit consent. Neither the financial institution ( referred to as "" Furnisher of information to credit agencies '' ) nor the consumer reporting agencies, XXXX and XXXX, possess my consent to furnish my information, nor do they have my written authorization. 

Furthermore, Section 6802 ( b ) ( c ) emphasizes that a financial institution can not disclose nonpublic personal information to nonaffiliated third parties without providing the consumer an explanation of how they can exercise their right to nondisclosure. In this case, the financial institution failed to inform me of my nondisclosure option, as required by law.

Additionally, Section 1681C ( a ) ( 5 ) prohibits consumer reporting agencies from including adverse information in a consumer report without authorization, unless authorized under subsection ( b ). The adverse item being reported without my consent violates this provision.

